  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSpouses who both earn compensation income cannot file a joint income tax return; they must file separate returns based on their respective total taxable income.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis is governed by Section 24 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that married individuals compute their income tax separately based on their individual incomes.\n\n### Interpretation\nAccording to Section 24 of the NIRC, each spouse is required to report their income separately unless the income cannot be distinctly attributed to either spouse, in which case it is divided equally for tax purposes. This provision emphasizes the principle of separate taxation for married individuals earning compensation income.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practice, this means that each spouse must file their own income tax return, detailing their individual earnings and deductions. This can affect tax planning strategies, as the combined income may push them into a higher tax bracket if considered together, but they must adhere to the requirement of separate filings.",
